  <div class="translator-box">
    <div class="translator-label">✦ Translator's Summary — Halacha Two</div>

    <p>This halacha draws on Likutay I:34 and introduces an entirely new angle on <em>Chalah</em> — not through the lens of holy speech (<em>leshon hakodesh</em>) as in Halacha One, but through the lens of the holy point (<em>nekudah hakedoshah</em>), the <em>tzaddik</em>, and the rectification of the heart.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Section 1 (§א) — The General Principle:</strong> The holy point = <em>tzaddik yesod olam</em> = <em>bris shalom</em> = the seat of holy love. The <em>tzaddik</em> holds the universal point; every individual point receives from him. His illumination nullifies shames (<em>charafos</em>) and brokenness of heart (<em>shvirat leiv</em>) afflicting Israel.</li>

      <li><strong>Section 2 (§ב) — Chalah is the Point Hidden in the Bread:</strong> Bread = <em>sa'ada d'liba</em> (support of the heart — Rashi on Tehillim 104:15). The Shattering introduced shames into the heart wherever bread should be giving life. <em>Chalah</em> = <em>reishis</em> = <em>kodesh</em> = the holy point concealed within the dough. The <em>Tikkunim</em>: <em>"Bereishis = a point in its chamber."</em> Sages: <em>"In the merit of Chalah the world was created."</em> Separating it and raising it to the Kohen (the universal point) reveals the holy love, nullifies the shames, and rectifies the bread. Kohenhood itself depends on <em>shmirat habris</em> — for it is beside the <em>bris</em> that holy love dwells.</li>

      <li><strong>Section 3 (§ג) — Shabbos and Chalah:</strong> Shabbos = the holy point = <em>yud</em> = the covenant = <em>oneg Shabbos</em> = holy love. On Shabbos, all six weekdays ascend and are covered by the Shabbos-point; their shames dissolve. Hence the custom (brought in Shulchan Aruch, Orach Chaim, beginning of Hilchos Shabbos) to bake Jewish bread specifically for Shabbos in order to separate <em>Chalah</em>.</li>

      <li><strong>Section 4 (§ד) — Women, the Heart, and Blessing:</strong> Woman = receiver = student = heart. Kohen/Rav = influencer = wisdom. Awakening from below (<em>is'arusa diletata</em>) is required: the woman initiates by separating <em>Chalah</em> and raising it to the Kohen, thereby drawing the point's illumination into her heart. This repairs her role in the Tree of Knowledge sin which introduced the <em>shvirah</em>. Proof: Eliyahu asked the Tzarfatite woman for a <em>ugah</em> (= <em>Chalah</em>) — and blessing filled her flour and oil.</li>

      <li><strong>Section 5 (§ה) — Omer, No Minimum, and Eretz Yisrael:</strong> Omer = 1⁄10 ephah = <em>yud</em> = the point — when dough reaches this size, the point-illumination is present and obligation arises. <em>Chalah</em> has no minimum separation size, since a point has no dimension. Essential Biblical obligation is in Eretz Yisrael — where the point shines most fully — culminating in the Even Shesiyah in the Holy of Holies, the primordial point from which the world was founded.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>Comparison with Halacha 1:</em> Halacha 1 reads <em>Chalah</em> through <em>leshon hakodesh / da'as / chochmah</em>. Halacha 2 reads it through <em>nekudah / ahavah dikedushah / bris / leiv</em>. Both converge: the Kohen holds the root; separating <em>Chalah</em> and raising it to him reconnects the scattered sparks — whether of speech or of the heart — to their holy source.</p>
